    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Cold winters can be annoying for people who wear glasses. Almost every time they come indoors, their glasses fog up. A new type of "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "coating",
            "zh": "涂层"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " made from tree wastes could be the "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b1",
            "number": 1
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "When you enter a house from the cold outdoors, the warm air around you "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b2",
            "number": 2
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". This causes some of the water vapour to become very small drops of water. If you wear glasses, they will land on the "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "lenses",
            "zh": "镜片"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " and fog them up, Alexander Henn explains. The researcher at Aalto University in Espoo, Finland, "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b3",
            "number": 3
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " the new coating."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The shape of the water drops on the lenses makes it hard to see, says Monika Österberg, another scientist from Aalto University. Water drops scatter light in all directions, she explains. When you look through them, you can’t focus on anything. But all is "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b4",
            "number": 4
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " when you look through a thin "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "film",
            "zh": "薄膜"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of water."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Österberg and Henn solved the problem using "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "lignin",
            "zh": "木质素"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". This material can be found in all woody plants. It’s what keeps them "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "stiff",
            "zh": "不易弯曲的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " and strong."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Here in Finland, we have a lot of trees,” Österberg says. “Everyone knows that you can build with "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b5",
            "number": 5
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". You can use it to make paper or, now, "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "textiles",
            "zh": "纺织品"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ",” she says. But most of those processes don’t use lignin from trees. It ends up as a "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b6",
            "number": 6
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " product."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Waste lignin is a brown powder that "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "resembles",
            "zh": "看起来像"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " cocoa. It is often burnt as a fuel, but the scientists believed there were "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b7",
            "number": 7
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " ways to use it."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "They "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "dissolved",
            "ref": "v-dissolve"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " lignin powder in a special liquid. When they poured that liquid into water, the lignin came together. Tiny bits of it formed and spread over the water. Those bits were "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "hydrophilic",
            "zh": "亲水的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", which "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b8",
            "number": 8
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " they loved water, Henn says."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p8",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Henn realised that coating glass with water-loving lignin bits would "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b9",
            "number": 9
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " how its surface "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "interacts",
            "ref": "v-interact"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " with water. The tiny "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "particles",
            "zh": "颗粒"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " want to grab onto the water "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "molecules",
            "zh": "分子"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", spreading them across the glass surface. If that water spreads out enough, the water drops would form a "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "continuous",
            "ref": "v-continuous"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " thin layer . Now wet, the lenses might "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b10",
            "number": 10
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " be foggy."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
